100 answerable cases, one set of replies, four ways of scoring.

  metric                     score   what it counts as right
  exact match                  0%   the reply is exactly the answer
  contains the answer         96%   the answer is somewhere in the reply
  token F1                    22%   word overlap with the answer
  embedding similarity        42%   the reply means something similar

Exact match scores 0%, and the system is not that bad —
it answers in sentences, and a sentence is never equal to a number. Exact
match is the right metric only when the output has been constrained to be
exactly the answer, which Chapter 8 shows how to do and this system does not.

Token F1 gives 22%, which looks like a score and is not one. It rewards a
long reply for containing common words and punishes a correct short one for
containing few.

Embedding similarity is the interesting failure. Look at what it does to the
96 answers the string rule calls correct:

  lowest similarity among correct answers   0.10
  highest                                   0.68
  mean                                      0.43
  mean among wrong answers                  0.16   (only 4 of them)

Correct answers span 0.10 to 0.68, and the number is not comparable across
questions: a short exact reply to a long question scores low because it is
short, not because it is wrong.

This run produced only 4 wrong answers, which is far too few to fit a threshold
to. That is worth saying rather than quietly fitting one anyway — with a
handful of negatives, any cut-off you compute describes a handful of
sentences.

Two correct answers from the bottom of the range:

  scored 0.29
    Q: What is the racked storage capacity at the Dallas depot?
    A: The Dallas depot’s racked storage capacity is **54,100 sq ft**.
    gold: 54,100
  scored 0.30
    Q: Why does the company say Halloway Group left?
    A: The company says Halloway Group left because it **did not renew 
    gold: competitor

Similarity measures topic and register, not truth. Use it to rank near misses
worth reading. Do not use it to decide whether you shipped a regression.
